{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_120","type":"agent_message","text":"decision: changes-requested\n\nThe focused policy tests, publication test under the race detector, and `go vet ./...` passed. Both fresh `pose validate` runs failed because the unit suite exceeded 600 seconds. The draft distinguishes those reruns from the bundle’s sealed passing evidence.\n\nRules applied: `pose-review`, the review workflow, backend-go, security, documentation-style, and delivery-evidence. No risks are accepted. The findings below are open blockers; criterion disposition `finding` means failed.\n\nThe entry-point sweep covered CLI close and recovery, review-check/verify/plan, closeout-check and MCP state, reached/nextCloses, obligations/attention, roadmap gates, federation, and doctor. They do not consistently enforce the same closeout contract. No adversarial-corpus case specifically covers the new receipt and exemption bypasses.\n\nThe assessment commands unexpectedly modified four generated files: `.pose/assessments/{integrations,technical-debt}.md` and `.pose/state/{integrations,technical-debt}.json`. Restoration was rejected by the read-only permission policy with approval unavailable. No source fix, commit, or attestation was made.\n\n```pose-draft\n{\n  \"schema\": \"pose.review.draft/v1\",\n  \"decision\": \"changes-requested\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F1\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_review_policy.go\",\n      \"line\": 303,\n      \"summary\": \"An ordinary scope configured with never is exempted without checking mandatory-human independence. protectCloseoutDecision only examines scopes changing governance paths, so a mandatory-human aggregate with no recorded review can close without human approval. Apply mandatory-human precedence before granting any exemption, as D1 and R5 require.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F2\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\",\n      \"line\": 2432,\n      \"summary\": \"An existing receipt causes close to return success without requiring state.Terminal. A valid later rejection, an open child, or another terminal-verification blocker can make the computed state nonterminal while this branch still reports that the receipt stands. Repeated close must verify the current applicable terminal contract before returning success.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\",\n      \"line\": 2449,\n      \"summary\": \"Recovery of a done roadmap without a receipt overwrites Review.Required with false whenever the raw policy evaluator returns an exemption, including when Review.Approved is false because of a recorded rejection. The later authorization check also permits receiptClose.Required=false. Thus never can publish a closeout receipt over a voluntary negative review, contrary to R5.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F4\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_review_policy.go\",\n      \"line\": 292,\n      \"summary\": \"Receipt decisions are trusted without verifying their recorded subject, policy or review bindings. SubjectDigest is only checked for nonemptiness and is never compared with the current semantic subject. Changing a closed milestone's composition to other already-terminal members therefore preserves its old exemption and terminal status. The parser also accepts an always decision with Required=false. Validate receipt integrity and semantic continuity before reusing its historical decision.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F5\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_review_policy.go\",\n      \"line\": 354,\n      \"summary\": \"Historical status is inferred solely from the roadmap currently saying done. This does not establish closure before adoption or a sealed historical contract. In particular, a new roadmap close interrupted after publishing status but before publishing its receipt is treated as historical; with an approved review, getCloseoutState can report terminal without the receipt. Its milestones also regain legacy inheritance despite adopted always. Establish historical provenance and keep interrupted adopted closes incomplete.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F6\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_closeout.go\",\n      \"line\": 1523,\n      \"summary\": \"The disabled-policy and legacy-cutoff returns precede the adopted closeout gate. A schema-5 always policy with enabled=false therefore returns Required=false and no CloseoutReview decision; getCloseoutState consequently does not set CloseRequired and can mark a milestone terminal without either its own review or a receipt. Adopted aggregate rules must take precedence over these legacy shortcuts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F7\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/federated_acceptance.go\",\n      \"line\": 701,\n      \"summary\": \"Federated source proof still requires a nonnil review bundle and attestation. The new exempt-milestone verification deliberately returns neither, while an exempt done roadmap falls back to its absent own review. A producer correctly closed under never is therefore rejected as source-review-not-approved. Implement the receipt-bound producer proof promised by the federation plan, preserving member evidence and source authority.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F8\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\",\n      \"line\": 2474,\n      \"summary\": \"Receipt publication compares the protected decision from closeoutReviewGate with the unprotected EvaluateCloseoutReview result. When baseline protection correctly restores Required=true over a current never exemption, even a fully approved scope always fails this comparison and cannot publish its receipt. Repeating close cannot repair it. Use the same protected gate for preflight and publication.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F9\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_closeout.go\",\n      \"line\": 1966,\n      \"summary\": \"Substituting an exemption for Review.Approved does not require the remaining closeout blockers to be clear. An exempt aggregate with terminal historical children can proceed despite a governance-read-view-unknown blocker. Publication then compares only Required, member identities and PolicyValue, not the complete authorization, subject, ADR inputs or read-view freshness, and creates the receipt from the earlier judged state. Refuse unknown inputs and revalidate all publication-relevant gates.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F10\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_review_policy.go\",\n      \"line\": 263,\n      \"summary\": \"A local transfer redirect returns its immediate Destination as canonical without resolving the destination through ArtifactResolver. In an A-to-B-to-C transfer chain, a local A alias counts as B while a direct or externally resolved alias counts as C. Destination authorization or availability is also not established here. Resolve to the final verified identity, with the existing cycle and depth checks, before counting members.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F11\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_adr_associations.go\",\n      \"line\": 138,\n      \"summary\": \"An ADR found through forward metadata or a Markdown link is skipped by the inventory before its contents are read, bounded or validated. regularADR only checks file metadata and containment. Consequently, adding a forward association can hide that ADR's unreadability, excessive size or malformed roadmaps declaration and turn an unknown result into Known=true. Validate associated ADR inputs as well as unassociated inventory entries.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F12\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_plan.go\",\n      \"line\": 404,\n      \"summary\": \"ReviewPlan silently discards closeoutReviewGate errors. For example, a corrupt receipt yields a successful plan with no CloseoutReview field or diagnostic, whereas closeout-check rejects it. Propagate the error or return an explicit blocking diagnostic so CLI and MCP planning disclose the same failure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F13\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/cli/surface_check.go\",\n      \"line\": 407,\n      \"summary\": \"The evaluator and milestoneMember accept spec:slug, but roadmapGate strips the type only for project-qualified references. An unqualified spec:slug becomes spec:spec:slug here, so both milestone and roadmap close fail their production gate for a member syntax explicitly required by R3. Normalize all supported local member references before invoking GetCloseoutState.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F14\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_review_policy.go\",\n      \"line\": 306,\n      \"summary\": \"The gate grants an exemption without requiring decision.Known. Under never, an unresolved external member produces Required=false and Known=false; ReviewCheck then reports review not required and the review-check/scopeApproved path succeeds. VerifyReviewBundle separately checks Known and refuses, creating inconsistent approval answers. Unknown member inputs must not produce a successful exemption.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F15\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"disposition\": \"blocker\",\n      \"file\": \"pose-mcp/internal/pose/closeout_review_policy.go\",\n      \"line\": 421,\n      \"summary\": \"Doctor skips any scope with a recorded approval before evaluating its current composition and review requirement. After composition changes make when-justified require a fresh aggregate review, an old stale approval suppresses the R6 warning even though close can reject it. Distinguish a recorded approval from one that satisfies the current obligation.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"criteria\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"assumption-integrity\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F5\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Verified the accepted thresholds and schema migration against D1/D2 and the focused tests. The material assumption that current done status proves pre-adoption sealed closure is unsupported. Receipt integrity and fresh-validation completion also remain unresolved; no risks are accepted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"backend-concurrency\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F9\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Inspected writer guards, atomic ExpectAbsent publication and publication rechecks. Atomic non-overwrite is present, but the exemption path does not establish complete, stable authorization before publishing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"backend-contracts\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F7\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The new receipt/exemption contract is not integrated with the existing federated proof consumer. F12 and F13 additionally identify inconsistent planning and member-reference contracts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"backend-input-errors\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F11\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Policy enum and pair validation are covered, but associated ADR read failures can be skipped. F12 and F14 identify additional error suppression and unknown-input inconsistencies.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"backend-integration-impact\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F7\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Traced producer closeout through federatedSourceProof. Legitimate exempt producer scopes cannot supply the bundle/attestation shape the consumer still requires.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"backend-observability\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F12\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Returned context generally names policy and counts, but ReviewPlan suppresses gate errors, and doctor can suppress currently owed reviews as described in F15.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"compatibility\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F7\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Schema-5 rejection by older engines is explicitly migrated by D1/D2, and legacy update compatibility has sealed evidence. Federation nevertheless lacks the required receipt-compatible proof path; supported typed member syntax also fails actual close in F13.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"correctness\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F3\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Focused tests pass, but the done-without-receipt recovery path can override a recorded negative review. F2, F4, F5, F6 and F8 identify additional incorrect terminal and close behavior.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"design-causality\",\n      \"disposition\": \"passed\",\n      \"evidence\": \"structure:design-399c4ac821a94d03\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Examined D1/D2, the ADR, evaluator, policy catalog and receipt design. Two explicit enums and deterministic composition thresholds address unnecessary aggregate review cost; separate receipts preserve mandatory explicit close. The implementation defects are recorded separately.\",\n      \"mappings\": [\n        {\n          \"delta\": \"SD-607ff681\",\n          \"basis\": \"D2\",\n          \"disposition\": \"mapped\",\n          \"rationale\": \"The roadmap template adds flat adrs metadata for the explicit ADR association required by R3, without introducing a global ADR ArtifactRef kind.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"delta\": \"SD-ec2d86f4\",\n          \"basis\": \"D1\",\n          \"disposition\": \"mapped\",\n          \"rationale\": \"The accepted ADR records policy location, modes, thresholds, prospective adoption, explicit close and preservation of negative reviews, satisfying the architectural decision required by R1.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"documentation\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F5\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Compared English and Portuguese manuals, roadmap templates and scaffold changes with runtime behavior. They promise receipt-required prospective closure and sealed historical verification, but runtime treats any done roadmap as historical.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"operability\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F8\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The protected-policy publication failure tells the operator to repeat close, but repetition cannot reconcile the protected and raw evaluator results. F12 and F15 further limit actionable diagnosis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"requirements\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F1\",\n      \"rationale\": \"R1/R2 have substantial schema, adoption and documentation coverage. R3-R7 are not fully met: mandatory-human precedence, negative-review preservation, receipt continuity, federation and doctor behavior have open findings. Several negative scenarios promised by the test plan are absent from the new tests.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"scope\",\n      \"disposition\": \"passed\",\n      \"evidence\": \"integration:pose-mcp/go/artifact-claim-boundary-integration\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Reviewed the sealed attributed artifact set against the spec's artifact declarations and D1/D2. The evaluator, receipt, policy/schema, projections, tests and documentation fit the approved feature scope. Interleaved work in the raw commit range is not treated as this spec's implementation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"security\",\n      \"disposition\": \"finding\",\n      \"evidence\": \"F1\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Inspected authority precedence, paths, redirects, receipt parsing and negative-review handling. Mandatory-human can be bypassed, existing receipts bypass terminal verification, and recovery can publish over a rejection. No accepted security risk is proposed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"solution-proportionality\",\n      \"disposition\": \"passed\",\n      \"evidence\": \"structure:design-399c4ac821a94d03\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Compared the design with boolean settings and merely relaxing existing review checks. Booleans cannot express the approved middle mode; relaxing checks alone cannot preserve mandatory explicit close. Reusing the catalog, resolver and publication machinery is proportionate. Local ADR metadata avoids speculative global discovery. This choice needs reconsideration if remote ADR discovery is required or schema 5 conflicts with another contract, as D2 states.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"tools\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"artifact-check\",\n      \"disposition\": \"passed\",\n      \"evidence\": \"command:pose artifact-check --spec pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit --strict\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Ran the exact required command; exit 0. Output included artifact.claims=37, artifact.observed=43 and artifact.findings=928, with warnings. Its first printed recorded change set used head 88ded5ef rather than the brief's head. A separate explicit raw-range check exited 1 and included interleaved other-spec paths; neither result is treated as independent proof that every raw-range path belongs to this scope.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"assess-design\",\n      \"disposition\": \"passed\",\n      \"evidence\": \"structure:design-399c4ac821a94d03\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Ran pose assess design --spec pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit --json. It returned status observed, the matching design input digest, and the two governance facts mapped under design-causality.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"assess-discover\",\n      \"component\": \"pose-mcp\",\n      \"disposition\": \"not-used\",\n      \"evidence\": \"command:pose context --task spec:pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit --json\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Skipped the recommended discovery producer to avoid further generated-state writes. Used the sealed component evidence and inspected the implementation directly.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"assess-integrate\",\n      \"disposition\": \"passed\",\n      \"evidence\": \"integration:pose-mcp/go/closeout-review-policy-integration\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Ran pose assess integrate: 70 contracts evaluated, 67 active, three unobserved HTTP-consumer gaps reported. The sealed feature integration check passes, but inspection found the untested producer-to-consumer receipt gap F7.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"assess-tech-debt\",\n      \"disposition\": \"failed\",\n      \"evidence\": \"command:pose assess tech-debt\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Executed successfully as a command, but its assessment reported techdebt.scan=INCOMPLETE because two historical spec sources could not be read. It reported two markers and one uncovered item. This is not evidence of complete debt coverage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"closeout-check\",\n      \"disposition\": \"deferred\",\n      \"evidence\": \"command:pose context --task spec:pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit --json\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Completion-only gate: defer until POSE records and v